---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import GCounterDemo from "../../components/GCounterDemo.astro";
import TermCallout from "../../components/TermCallout.astro";
import {
  eventualConsistencyTerm,
  replicaTerm,
} from "../../lib/atlas/standalone-terms";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Counters"
  description="Follow three friends counting birds on separate hikes. Their checkpoint notes arrive at different times, then a G-counter brings every view to the same total."
  territory="Structures"
  sheetTitle="Counters"
>
  <article class="structure-page">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="territory-label">Structures</p>
      <h1>Counters</h1>
      <p class="structure-summary">
        Alice, Bob, and Carol count birds on separate hikes. At each checkpoint,
        they leave a note with their name and running count. Copies of those
        notes reach other checkpoints at different times, so the three friends
        can temporarily see different totals.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="one-counter-title">
      <h2 id="one-counter-title">Alice starts counting birds</h2>
      <p>
        Alice takes the ridge trail. Each time she spots a bird, she adds one
        to her running count. With only Alice hiking, the shared total is simply
        the number she has seen.
      </p>
      <p>
        Alice's local copy of the shared counter is a{" "}
        <dfn><a href="/glossary/#replica">replica</a></dfn>. Bob and Carol each
        keep their own replica. The hikers exchange cumulative messages by
        leaving notes at known checkpoints.
      </p>
      <TermCallout {...replicaTerm} />
      <pre aria-label="Alice's bird count"><code>birds = count[Alice]</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="second-counter-title">
      <h2 id="second-counter-title">Bob takes a different trail</h2>
      <p>
        Bob counts birds along the river trail. Alice changes only Alice's
        count, and Bob changes only Bob's count. If Alice has seen 7 birds and
        Bob has seen 3, their shared total is 10.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a grow-only counter, or G-counter. Each replica keeps the
        largest note it has received from each hiker, then adds those counts.
        It does not add every message. If Bob leaves the same checkpoint note
        twice, both messages say that Bob has seen 3 birds. His count stays 3.
      </p>
      <pre aria-label="G-counter merge and bird total rules"><code>count[Alice] = max(all notes from Alice)
count[Bob] = max(all notes from Bob)
birds = count[Alice] + count[Bob] + ...</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="race-title">
      <h2 id="race-title">Their checkpoint notes cross</h2>
      <p>
        Before splitting up, the friends agree on known checkpoints where they
        can leave their latest count. Each hiker keeps a table in a notebook,
        with one row for Alice, Bob, and Carol. A row holds the largest count
        that hiker has reported so far. Adding the three rows gives the shared
        bird count.
      </p>
      <p>
        Alice leaves a note after seeing 7 birds. Bob leaves another after
        seeing 3. Carol has not counted a bird yet. When Carol finds those
        notes at different checkpoints, her notebook changes like this:
      </p>
      <figure class="notebook">
        <table aria-label="Carol's notebook at each checkpoint">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Checkpoint and note</th>
              <th scope="col">Alice</th>
              <th scope="col">Bob</th>
              <th scope="col">Carol</th>
              <th scope="col">Total</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Trailhead</th><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">North fork: Alice 7</th><td>7</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>7</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">River bridge: Bob 3</th><td>7</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>10</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Old lookout: Alice 4</th><td>7</td><td>3</td><td>0</td><td>10</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <figcaption>
          Each row shows Carol's notebook after she reads the note at that checkpoint.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        Carol might find Alice's note for 7, then Bob's note for 3, then an
        older Alice note for 4. The last note does not lower Alice's row:
        <code>max(7, 4) = 7</code>. If Carol finds 4 before 7, the row changes
        from 0 to 4 to 7. Both arrival orders produce the same notebook and the
        same total.
      </p>
      <p>
        Copies of the notes do not reach every checkpoint at once. Alice can
        see 7, Bob can see 3, and Carol can still see 0. Use the controls to
        leave one note at a time, or leave Alice's and Bob's notes together.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GCounterDemo />

    <section aria-labelledby="result-title">
      <h2 id="result-title">Every trail catches up</h2>
      <p>
        After both checkpoint notes arrive, Alice, Bob, and Carol all read
        10. Their views were temporarily inconsistent, but the G-counter kept
        both hikers' observations and made every replica agree.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is{" "}
        <dfn><a href="/glossary/#eventual-consistency">eventual consistency</a></dfn>.
        For each hiker, merge keeps the larger count from their checkpoint
        notes. The rule is commutative, associative, and idempotent. Arrival
        order does not change the answer, and the same checkpoint note can
        arrive more than once.
      </p>
      <TermCallout {...eventualConsistencyTerm} />
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="limits-title">
      <h2 id="limits-title">The bird count can only grow</h2>
      <p>
        Alice, Bob, and Carol can add sightings, but they cannot subtract one.
        A negative change is rejected before state changes. Every hiker also
        needs a unique replica ID. If two hikers share an ID, pairwise maximum
        can discard one concurrent count.
      </p>
      <table class="counter-family" aria-label="Counter family status">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Counter</th>
            <th scope="col">Use</th>
            <th scope="col">Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">G-counter</th>
            <td>Increment only</td>
            <td>Read now</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Operation counter</th>
            <td>Add delivered operations once</td>
            <td>Planned</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">PN-counter</th>
            <td>Increment and decrement</td>
            <td>Planned</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="notes-title">
      <h2 id="notes-title">Sources and implementation</h2>
      <p>
        This lesson calls Watershed's public{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/src/watershed/g_counter_kernel.gleam">G-counter kernel</a>.
        Its interaction follows Watershed's focused{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/website/src/components/Demo.astro">counter demo</a>
        , while Atlas owns the three-client lesson schedule and presentation.
        The merge rule keeps the largest cumulative count for each hiker.
      </p>
      <p>
        The merge rule is the state-based grow-only counter described in{" "}
        <a href="https://inria.hal.science/inria-00555588/document">A comprehensive study of Convergent and Commutative Replicated Data Types</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="structure-next" aria-label="Continue through structures">
      <a href="/structures/">Browse all structures</a>
      <a href="/atlas/multi-value-registers/">Continue to registers</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .structure-page {
    max-width: var(--measure-reading);
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .structure-page > section {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
  }

  .structure-summary {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  .structure-page > section > p dfn {
    color: var(--signal);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  .structure-page > section > p dfn a {
    color: inherit;
  }

  pre {
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border-block: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
    background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--clear) 45%, var(--sky-sheet));
  }

  .notebook {
    margin-block: var(--space-5);
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
    border-block: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .notebook table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 36rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }

  .notebook :is(th, td) {
    padding: var(--space-2) var(--space-3);
    border-bottom: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line-faint);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .notebook td {
    font-family: var(--font-data);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .notebook tbody tr:last-child :is(th, td) {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  .notebook figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  .counter-family {
    width: 100%;
    margin-block: var(--space-5);
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }

  .counter-family :is(th, td) {
    padding: var(--space-3);
    border-block: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }


  .counter-family th {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
  }

  .counter-family td:last-child {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  .structure-next {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
    padding-block: var(--space-5);
    border-top: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-weight: 650;
  }
</style>
